  "overview": {
    "text": "Hydrochloric acid (HCl) is an aqueous solution of hydrogen chloride, a gas; it is an inorganic, strong acid. In water, HCl dissociates essentially completely into hydronium and chloride ions, which explains its “strong acid” classification—strength refers to ionization, not simply how concentrated a sample is. In the body, gastric glands secrete hydrochloric acid as part of stomach acid, which helps digestion alongside enzymes. Beyond biology, it is an important industrial chemical: uses include cleaning oxide from steel (“pickling”), processing ores, producing other chemicals, and adjusting water pH. Hydrochloric acid is corrosive, so laboratory and industrial use requires appropriate controls.",
